    <h2>Glass. Workspace. Cloud.</h2>
    <p class="lead">Polaris begins where native screen mirroring ends. AirPlay, Miracast and Google Cast each hand the display to one device at a time. Polaris puts the <em style="color:#7ce3a8;font-style:normal">Mersive Workspace</em> behind them: a shared canvas where several sources sit side by side at once, arriving by native cast or straight from a browser. The workspace is not bound to the LAN. A guest sitting in the room, a colleague on another VLAN and someone on the other side of the world all reach the same room over the internet, with no VPN to stand up, no network to join and nothing inbound to open.</p>
    <p class="sdek">The canvas isn&rsquo;t only on the wall. app.mersive.com renders the same workspace for every seat, in the room or remote, so the people who aren&rsquo;t standing in front of the glass aren&rsquo;t watching a lesser view of it. And every display runs from one cloud, whether that is a single room or an estate of thousands. Three layers make it work:</p>
    <div class="clb-caps">
      <div class="clb-cap"><h3>The glass</h3><p>Any display you already own becomes a Polaris endpoint: a meeting room display, a classroom screen, a lobby wall.</p></div>
      <div class="clb-cap"><h3>The workspace</h3><p>Every share lands side by side on the display, from a laptop, a phone, or a browser with nothing installed, and from any network, not just the room&rsquo;s.</p></div>
      <div class="clb-cap"><h3>The cloud</h3><p>The broker and the fleet console: it introduces every session, runs every room, and stays out of the media path.</p></div>
    </div>

